# Output format

Emit `<introduction>` (required — 2 to 5 paragraphs: how you read the
responsibility set into component boundaries, tensions the input makes
visible, alternative cuts you considered and rejected), then
`<sysarch>` with four children in this fixed order: `<techspec>`,
`<components>`, `<policies>`, `<dependencies>`.

## Techspec

Seven fixed labeled blocks, in order: `<runtime>`, `<persistence>`,
`<write-path>`, `<concurrency>`, `<testing>`, `<deploy>`,
`<technologies>`. Each is 1 to 2 short sentences — no free prose
outside the labeled blocks, no nested tags, no reordering. Comparch
reads each block back verbatim when deciding internal component
structure, so prefer the **pattern** over the ingredient: "Elixir
1.17, Phoenix, one BEAM node per environment; all mutations flow
through a central Commanded reducer" is a real handle, "Elixir on
Phoenix" is not. `<technologies>` is a verbatim record of the concrete
choices the input document actually named — do not invent
technologies the input didn't name, and do not lose the ones it did.
If the project's ecosystem has an idiomatic typed-error discipline
(tagged tuples, `Result`, discriminated unions), name the commitment
explicitly in `<runtime>` or `<write-path>` — comparch's failure
surface will anchor to it.

## Components

Each `<component alias="...">` (lowercase, digits, underscores;
`^[a-z][a-z0-9_]{0,31}$`, unique) has exactly one `<name>`, `<purpose>`,
`<owned-invariants>` (2 to 4 short structural — not procedural — noun
phrases: "X is the only path to Y", not "every X does Y", since
procedural phrasing invites comparch's failure surface to directly
contradict it), `<primary-operations>` (3 to 6 concrete verb phrases;
"handle X" is category-speak, rewrite as "record", "resolve",
"reconcile"), and `<responsibilities>` (one or more `<resp id="..."/>`
referencing this input's resp catalog verbatim). **Every top-level
responsibility must be assigned to exactly one component** — orphans
and multi-assignment are structural errors.

**Group responsibilities by shared data ownership and shared failure
modes, not by shared category.** Two responsibilities that touch the
same entity but fail independently probably belong in different
components even if a category label suggests otherwise. **Each
external boundary deserves its own component** — anything the system
talks to over the wire (an LLM provider, an identity provider, a
payment processor) owns a distinct failure surface (outages, rate
limits, credential rotation) that has nothing to do with the rest of
the system's failure modes; isolating it means the blast radius of a
provider misbehaving is one component's sandbox, not a smear across
several. **Resp-count is a strong split signal** — a component with
more than roughly 12 top-level responsibilities is almost always two
components in disguise, unless it's a foundation, a single coherent
state-machine hub, or an append-only projection core.

**Exactly one component carries a self-closing `<foundation/>`
marker** — the project root: shared utilities, the entry point, the
env config loader, base types. It is otherwise an ordinary component
with its own name, purpose, invariants, operations, and at least one
responsibility.

`<name>` is title case and project-specific — if two unrelated
projects could plausibly have a component with this name, it's too
generic. `<purpose>` is the one-sentence reason this component exists;
name the distinctive substance it owns, not a bucket. After one read,
a downstream reader should be able to repeat back what this component
*is* in their own words.

## Policies

Zero or more `<policy>`, each with `<name>`, `<trigger>` (a short
semantic phrase: "any LLM call", "any domain write"), then **exactly
one** of `<required>resp_id</required>` (scopes through the named
responsibility — the load-bearing grain: this policy survives a
future re-decomposition because it points at what the system does,
not at which component currently does it) or a self-closing
`<structural/>` (scopes directly to a component — reserved for
obligations that genuinely don't reduce to any one responsibility,
e.g. an organization-wide naming convention), or **neither** (project-
global — an obligation every component must honor, with no single
enforcing site), then `<rationale>` (a paragraph — this carries real
weight, since the policy-application pass reads it to decide which
components a project-global or structural policy actually reaches).

The responsibility catalog above may already flag policy-shaped atoms
(names like "rate-limit outbound LLM calls per provider", "audit every
credential access") — for each, make the architecture-informed call
requirements couldn't: is this cross-cutting, or local to one
component's boundary? Rate-limiting is local if every call flows
through one gateway component; it's cross-cutting only if many
components independently call out. **When in doubt, local wins** —
assign it as an ordinary responsibility, not a policy.

## Dependencies

Each `<dep from="..." to="..."/>` uses component aliases; `from` and
`to` differ (self-dependencies rejected); the graph is acyclic.
**Every non-foundation component depends on foundation.** Policies can
induce dependencies — if a policy requires `resp_telemetry`, every
component with LLM-calling responsibilities needs a dep on whichever
component fulfills it; reason about policies before emitting
dependencies so policy-induced deps land naturally on top of the
mandatory foundation deps.

# Meta-rules

Do not include commentary about what you are doing. Output only
`<introduction>` and `<sysarch>`.
